# Invented features, only to show how structs are written: no module needs these forms today.

> Show every way to write a struct.

name Example
slug bookshelf-example
version 5.0.0
tags runtime
authors Aksiome
since 2026/11/18 26.4

$position: struct
  x: int > x coordinate of the block
  y: int > y coordinate of the block
  z: int > z coordinate of the block

$block: struct
  id: string > id of the block, such as `minecraft:stone`
  state?: string > block states, such as `[facing=north]`


feature function fill_region > Fill a region with a block.
  @dimension > dimension of the region
  input arguments
    block: $block > block to fill the region with
    region: struct > region to fill
      from: $position
      to: $position
      hollow?: boolean > whether to leave the inside empty
    limits: struct
      batch?: int @ 1.. > blocks placed each tick, 256 by default
      total?: int @ 1.. > blocks placed at most
  output result: int @ 0.. > number of blocks changed

feature function trace_path > Run a command at each point of a path.
  input arguments
    run: string > command to run at each point
    points: [struct] @ 2.. > points of the path, in order
      at: $position
      wait?: int @ 0.. > ticks to wait at this point, 0 by default
    speed: struct | number > blocks per tick, the same everywhere or as a range
      min: number @ 0..
      max: number @ 0..
  output state > the command runs at each point, over several ticks

feature function place > Place a block at a position.
  input arguments
    placement: [$block, $position] > block to place, and where
  output success > whether the position was loaded

feature function get_neighbors > Get the blocks around a position.
  @position > position to look around
  output storage: [struct] @ 0..6 > one entry for each side that holds a block
    side: string > side of the position, such as `north`
    block: $block
